If a clinician starts a methadone-treatment for a patient then the clinician should consider a shorter-follow-up-schedule for the patient.
If a clinician increases a methadone-dosage for a patient then the clinician should consider a shorter-follow-up-schedule for the patient.
If a clinician starts a methadone-treatment for a patient and the methadone-treatment has a therapy-start and the clinician uses a shorter-follow-up-schedule for the patient then the clinician conducts a follow-up with the patient at an interval of at least 2 days at an interval of at most 3 days during a window of 1 week of the therapy-start.
If a clinician increases a methadone-dosage for a patient and the methadone-dosage has a dosage-increase and the clinician uses a shorter-follow-up-schedule for the patient then the clinician conducts a follow-up with the patient at an interval of at least 2 days at an interval of at most 3 days during a window of 1 week of the dosage-increase.
